  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nFor the overnight hours and early this morning, winds are very\nslowly decreasing in speed. However, they will remain elevated\nfor the Arrowhead and North Shore, which will largely help impede\nany frost formation there. Where the winds have cut off, very\nspotty temperatures down to freezing have already been observed.\nFrost potential is expected to expand in coverage over more areas\ntowards the early commute hours this morning and especially by\nsunrise. All previously issued frost and freeze headlines remain\nin effect until 9 AM. After sunrise, a short-lived uptick in\ngusty north-northwest winds is expected for far northern\nMinnesota. Downsloping valleys on the North Shore could see a few\nhours of wind gusts to 30 mph through mid-day today before winds\nquickly become nearly calm by late afternoon.\n\nFor tonight, a more limited coverage of frost and freeze\nconditions is expected, starting late this evening and lasting\nthrough the pre-dawn hours Saturday. A second round of Frost\nAdvisories is in effect for the tip of the Arrowhead, as well as\nparts of northern Wisconsin. North Shore communities right along\nthe shorelines are not expected to see frost, with lows in the\nupper 30s compared to the low 30s for the terrain ridge and\nfurther inland. Iron County has an 80-90% chance of seeing sub-\nfreezing temperatures Friday night so this is the sole county\nwith a Freeze Warning for Friday night. Incoming cloud cover \nfrom the west will limit how much temperatures can bottom out\nfor most of northeast Minnesota, and better mixing from\nincreasing southerly winds creates no frost concerns for the\nBrainerd Lakes to Leech Lake region Friday night.\n\nThis incoming sky cover is associated with a shortwave moving\nalong the International Border later Friday night into the\ndaytime hours of Saturday. We will see increasing light and\nspotty rain shower chances of 30 to 50 percent for the Iron Range\nnorthward early Saturday morning. Highly localized spots could\nsee up to a couple of tenths of an inch of rain. As the\nassociated cold front moves over the region later Saturday\nafternoon, these rain shower chances will spread southward along\nthe front, finally reaching northwest Wisconsin by Saturday\nevening.\n\nA trailing and much cooler airmass aloft will move in on\nnortherly flow for Sunday. Breezy northerly winds gusting 25 to\n30 mph from mid-day into the early evening. As these winds \nslowly decrease Sunday evening, ideal radiational cooling will \nset up. This brings the best chances of this entire stretch for \na freeze potential to most of the region late Sunday night into\nearly Monday morning. Widespread freeze warning headlines may \nbe needed in time for this period; the exception might still be\nthe Brainerd Lakes area into central MN remaining only into the\nmid-30s.\n\nHeading into the next work week, temperatures will slowly warm \nas the overall flow shifts back to a southerly component. Highs \nwill climb into the upper 60s, scraping the 70s by mid-week. \nWhile a few periods of very light and spotty rain showers cannot\nbe ruled out, there is only a 10-20 percent chance of this \noccuring so the extended forecast is largely dry and pleasant.",
